WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.880
Colaberry's AI internship used to start with an email to two inboxes. This is the pipeline that replaced it.

2
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:23.320
An applicant records their consents, answers one question bank by web form or by an AI phone call, and a reviewer decides. Then come the offer documents, a payment step and a place in the internship cohort.

3
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.850
This is the reviewer's queue. Applicants' names and contact details are hidden in this walkthrough.

4
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:47.130
The Decide panel puts requirement checks, computed straight from the answers, beside an AI recommendation. The AI never scores or ranks. Only the reviewer can admit.

5
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:58.700
Every step is a transition in one table that names who may take it. Admission belongs to the reviewer alone, and there is no switch that lets the system do it.

6
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:08.550
Answers pulled from a phone interview are stored as needing follow-up, and only the applicant's own confirmation turns them into answers.

7
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:21.690
Once admitted, each intern is tracked in one console. Unknown is shown as unknown, never as zero, and nudges come from a fixed set of templates.

8
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:35.730
On live traffic, all eight admission decisions were made by a reviewer. The reviewer approved two of the three applicants the AI flagged for a closer look, and all forty two signed documents were verified.

9
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:44.570
Fourteen interns are active with no duplicate memberships. The pipeline was built in ninety four commits over ten weeks.

10
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:55.880
Nothing in the platform records whether an intern was hired, so placement is still unmeasured. The record says so plainly.

11
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.610
The AI does the work that scales, and a person keeps every admission. That is the part worth copying.
